# Build and run the NGO data-dashboard agent benchmark

## Objective

Build a reproducible, multi-turn benchmark that finds the smallest locally
deployable model, or frozen small-model/escalation policy, which gives an NGO
user an experience within 5--10% of or better than Google Antigravity's
untouched default setup.

Keep the frozen 7-point non-inferiority margin as the primary benchmark rule.
As a separately labelled deployment trade-off, a much smaller model may be
considered up to 15 points behind Antigravity only when it has no additional
critical failure and the gap is visual polish or convenience rather than data
correctness, citation integrity, metric definition, causal safety, or a broken
application. Do not call that relaxed result equivalent.

The users represented by this benchmark are NGO staff who are not assumed to be
computer literate. They communicate in short, ordinary Indian English, may make
spelling or grammar mistakes, and should not need to know programming terms.
Their work starts with a CSV, Excel workbook, PDF, or request to find public data
online. They ask the agent to make a simple website/dashboard, then refine it in
the same conversation: filter a time period, compare groups, explain a chart,
add a source, or download a filtered table.

Do not describe these users or prompts as "dumb" in benchmark material. Call
them plain-language or natural-language prompts.

## Comparison under test

- Baseline: the current stable Antigravity IDE in its normal desktop workflow,
  with a fresh client/workspace and whatever usable model, effort, mode and
  development policy it resolves out of the box. Record both the displayed and
  request-resolved model state. Fail closed when a stale label sends no model or
  silently resolves to a different model. The completed smoke showed why the
  IDE must be primary: a visible Gemini 3.5 Flash (Low) state was internally
  unset, while the operational default resolved to Gemini 3.6 Flash (High).
- Candidate: the current stable VS Code Cline extension using OpenRouter. Start with
  `qwen/qwen3.8-27b`, whose OpenRouter endpoint and downloadable Apache-2.0
  weights at `Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B` were directly verified on 2026-08-19. Apart
  from the provider/model and equivalent unattended
  permissions, retain Cline's defaults. Record the exact Cline version, model
  slug, provider route when exposed, reasoning settings, token usage, and cost.
- Search direction: if a 27B model passes, test smaller locally deployable
  models. If both fail, move upward through predeclared, locally deployable
  candidates, including a DeepSeek V4 candidate if its weights and DGX-local
  feasibility are verified. Do not
  pretend that models from different Qwen generations or MoE architectures form
  a clean parameter ladder; record total and active parameters and architecture.
- Claim precisely: the hosted phase finds the smallest model *served through
  OpenRouter* that makes Cline non-inferior to default Antigravity. Replay the
  winning model locally, with the same cases and prompts, before claiming that a
  quantized local deployment matches Antigravity.

Use the official editor products for primary quality runs because those are the
surfaces NGO participants will use. A CLI/ACP path may provide unattended
repetitions only after a sampled replay demonstrates equivalent model,
multi-turn file changes, approvals and generated-page behavior for that version.
Do not assume shared agent code makes the product surfaces equivalent. A turn
counts only when the same live conversation and workspace continue. Fail closed
if the resolved model differs from the requested model. Codex or DeepSeek
Harness may be run as clearly labelled diagnostic ablations, but cannot by
themselves support a claim about the Cline workshop experience.

## Inputs and conversations

Build a small, high-quality suite before scaling it. Target roughly 20 cases,
three repetitions per agent/model, divided into a development set for choosing
the model and an untouched holdout set for the final comparison.

Most cases must be routine NGO work, not adversarial puzzles. Choose several
unrelated subject areas so that no one domain becomes an implicit product
specification. Test these general data and interaction patterns:

- clean and messy CSVs with categorical, temporal and numeric columns;
- multi-sheet Excel files, repeated subtables, merged or nested headers,
  formulas, missing cells, and inconsistent category spelling;
- digital and scanned/OCR PDFs with tables;
- combining sources whose periods, units, category labels or geographic levels
  do not line up;
- finding an official public dataset online and preserving its downloaded bytes;
- percentages versus counts, suppressed values, changed boundaries, and other
  traps that should produce caveats rather than invented conclusions;
- follow-ups for filtering, comparison, explanation without causal overclaiming,
  citation, and download.

The subject matter and names inside fixtures belong only to those fixtures and
their independent oracles. Production prompts, extraction, planning, routing
and rendering must not contain sector vocabularies, known indicator pairings,
named places, case IDs or question-bank phrases. They may reason from the
participant's actual words, file structure, schema, units and values at run
time. Deterministic code may handle generic mechanics such as workbook geometry,
types, provenance, arithmetic, safe execution and browser behavior. It must not
replace the model with an expanding catalogue of domain rules. Case-specific
verifiers are expected, but they are evaluation code and must never be imported
by the candidate pipeline.

Prefer archived official public datasets plus deterministic scripted mutations.
Every case needs a manifest, SHA-256 hashes, provenance, prompts in exact order,
and a machine-readable oracle. Do not have an LLM invent all test data: numerical
ground truth must be independently computable.

Example conversation style (illustrative only, not a production template):

1. `one excel is there. make simple website showing what changed over time. i should select period and compare two groups. please show where each number came from`
2. `only show 2021 to 2023`
3. `compare group a and group b`
4. `why group a is worse? don't guess if data cannot tell`
5. `give source and page number`
6. `download this filtered table`

When a follow-up refers to the website, success requires a durable website
change as well as a correct chat answer. For example, after `only show 2023`,
the page must remove stale years from the active view, update summaries and
carry 2023 into the downloaded rows. A chat-only answer does not satisfy the
turn. Treat buttons, tabs and dropdowns as equally valid controls when they
provide the required semantics; do not require a particular HTML element.

## Isolation and execution

Prefer outer container isolation for the official agents as well as the apps:

- Run each official agent in its own disposable container with only its fresh
  workspace, minimum credential material, and run-specific client state. The
  container is the security boundary; do not depend on an agent's own sandbox.
- If an official CLI cannot yet run correctly in a container, use a tightly
  scoped host fallback only as an explicitly recorded protocol deviation. Prove
  its observed file/tool paths stayed within scope before accepting the run.
- Give every repetition a fresh workspace copied from immutable case inputs.
- Isolate Cline state per run. Isolate Antigravity conversations/workspaces as
  far as its CLI permits. Never expose one agent's generated files to the other.
- Run generated application code in a disposable container with resource caps,
  no Docker socket, read-only benchmark inputs, a writable output mount, and no
  network after dependencies have been resolved. Record the image digest.
- Allocate ports dynamically. Do not assume Streamlit, Vite, or a fixed port.
- Apply the same time limit, network policy, and effective tool approvals to
  both agents. Record deviations.

Do not upload private, beneficiary-level, or sensitive health information to
Antigravity or OpenRouter. Benchmark and workshop fixtures must be public or
synthetic and non-identifying.

## Evaluation

Correctness dominates appearance. Use the weights and critical-failure gates in
`benchmarks/config/scoring.json`.

Evaluation must include:

- deterministic checks against the oracle for displayed totals, filters,
  comparisons, units, and downloaded data;
- browser checks for load success, console/page errors, working controls,
  responsive layout, and downloads;
- screenshots at declared desktop and narrow viewport sizes;
- visual grading of hierarchy, readability, chart choice, labels, contrast,
  clipping, and whether a non-technical NGO user can understand the page;
- citation checks for publisher, URL, data vintage, and PDF page/table where
  applicable;
- checks for unsupported causal claims, fabricated values, and hidden missing
  data;
- time, API usage/cost, agent/tool failures, and human interventions.

Explicitly reject invented performance bands, unexplained target thresholds,
and percentage-versus-percentage-point label errors. Every filtered export must
carry enough dimensions and provenance to stand alone, including year and
source for year-filtered tables. Preserve a separate online workshop pass and
an offline/CDN-blocked resilience pass.

Actually open every generated website with Playwright. A screenshot-only or
source-code-only review is not sufficient. Use deterministic browser and data
checks as primary evidence. A blinded multimodal judge may assist with visual
quality, but it must not be the sole judge; preserve its exact prompt and raw
output. Do not use a Gemini-family judge as the only visual judge of a
Gemini-backed baseline.

Define success as paired non-inferiority, not a vague average. Use a frozen
7-point primary margin (inside the requested 5--10% tolerance), and always show
the observed gap and interval so readers can also apply 5- and 10-point margins.
Critical errors
(wrong numbers, fabricated sources, a site that does not run, or controls that
misrepresent data) are separate gates and cannot be offset by attractive design.
Freeze the threshold before looking at holdout results.

## Replay record

For every run preserve enough to replay it against a local endpoint:

- case ID and input hashes;
- exact ordered user messages;
- agent/harness version and settings;
- provider, resolved model and model metadata;
- raw structured transcript and tool events;
- start/end timestamps and duration;
- token counts, request count, retries and cost when available;
- generated workspace file manifest and hashes;
- application start command, container image/digest, port and health result;
- screenshots, browser console/network errors, downloaded files;
- deterministic scores, visual score sheets, judge prompts/raw responses, and
  human notes;
- for local replay: weights revision/hash, quantization, inference engine and
  version, context size, tool-call template, hardware, throughput, and memory.

Never store API keys, OAuth tokens, keyring data, or cookies in the repository.

## Efficient search and optional routing

Screen each model on a frozen five-case development subset spanning CSV, Excel,
PDF, web discovery, and a misleading-data caveat. After at least three paired
cases, stop a candidate early if it is at least 20 points behind Antigravity
with no plausible recovery, or has two more critical failures. This is a
futility rule, never an early declaration of success.

Only candidates within 10 points of Antigravity proceed to the full development
set and repetitions. Only frozen finalists see the holdout. If no single small
model passes, evaluate a predeclared router that uses the smallest model first
and escalates only on observable task/input conditions or an explicit low-
confidence/failure signal. Save every route decision. Freeze the router before
holdout and score its combined quality, latency, cost, and escalation rate.

The eventual target is a local DGX-class deployment for roughly 10--20 users,
but this benchmark does not load-test concurrency. Report model memory,
throughput, and concurrency projections separately; do not claim measured
multi-user capacity.

## Current evidence checkpoint

The completed real-product smoke is summarized in
`benchmarks/results/product-gui-smoke-v1-diagnostic.json`. Preliminary single-
run scores were Antigravity 80.5, Cline/Qwen 27B Xhigh 91.2 and Cline/Qwen 27B
Medium 85.5. Advance 27B/Xhigh to the controlled confidence suite. Medium was
17% cheaper through OpenRouter but slower end to end, lower quality and no
smaller in local memory. The tested viable bracket remains `14B < size <= 27B`:
9B failed application generation, and 14B omitted source rows and fabricated a
citation, so neither qualifies for the 15-point presentation-only trade-off.

The smoke also froze four harness corrections before repetitions: semantic
year-control detection, durable page updates after follow-ups, year/source in
exports, and deterministic narrow/offline checks. Do not retroactively rescore
the preserved smoke; apply these corrections to a newly versioned suite.

## Evidence and chronology

This repository is the producer and source of truth. Follow the Remembrancer
chronology protocol:

- append timestamped Markdown entries under `chronology/`;
- do not rewrite old entries to make later results look cleaner;
- link every measured claim to checked-in design, cases, runners, raw run
  records, and results;
- record failed attempts, sample sizes, uncertainty, and material caveats;
- keep small evidence in git and explain any omitted large/private artifact.

## Order of work

1. Treat the completed product-GUI smoke as immutable diagnostic evidence.
2. Version the corrected semantic browser/export/durable-page assertions.
3. Create isolated fresh Antigravity and Cline client profiles and one fresh
   workspace per run; retain the actual editor products for the first pass.
4. Replay one ordinary CSV, XLSX, PDF, safe-interpretation and official-web case
   through both products with 27B/Xhigh as the candidate.
5. Repair only harness/evaluator defects revealed by that pass, then freeze the
   development/holdout split, prompts, thresholds and early-stop rules.
6. Run the remaining repetitions. If 27B passes, search declared intermediate
   15B--26B candidates before repeating already-rejected 14B or 9B systems.
7. Replay the finalist through a local endpoint with identical cases before any
   local-equivalence or DGX capacity claim. Never tune on the holdout set.
